    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90601.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "Well, first of all, because those who were in uniform or in other posts six years ago are now civilians. So they can speak out and say that they can voice their opinions without having to resign or to be fired for doing so. And that's one reason. The other reason is I think that with time, even those who were both put it in the overview In May 2018, when Donald Trump withdrew from the Iran deal, I think a lot of them at that point thought that that wasn't a good thing, even if they hadn't been in favor of the deal originally. But I think that even those who were quite skeptical in 2015, by 2018, they saw the deal at least as something which was keeping Iran in check when it came to the main parts of nuclear development. The moment Trump withdrew from the head, there was no plan B. The people were saying, OK, we didn't like their own deal. But now there's no round deal. And there's chaos. There's nothing which can control Iran. And the alternative that Trump and his team with Netanyahu's backing presented of the maximum pressure sanctions wasn't seen by many Israelis at the time. It's only not now as a sufficient alternative to the various safeguards that were in the deal.",
